# Unit 5: Designing Reliable Apps with AI and APIs

## What is Designing Reliable Apps with AI and APIs all about?

#### In Designing Reliable Apps with AI and APIs, students learn how web apps use external APIs to fetch data and integrate AI-powered services into their programs.

#### Building on their earlier work with asynchronous JavaScript, arrays, and objects, they design apps that respond gracefully to loading delays, errors, and missing data, while considering how reliance on external systems affects reliability, user trust, and app behavior.


---

#### ***Note: Unlike the first semester, the second semester of AI Foundations is a continuous sequence and must be taught in order. Designing Reliable App unit in the course.***



## Student Learning Goals

<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; background: #f7f8fa; padding: 20px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.6; max-width: 800px; margin: 20px auto;">

<div>
  <h4 style="margin-top: 0; font-weight: bold;">By the end of this unit, students will be able to :</h4>
  <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 10px; font-size: 14pt">
    <li>
      Write asynchronous JavaScript using <code>async/await</code> to fetch data from an external API and display it in a web app.
    </li><br>
    <li>
      Use <code>try/catch</code> blocks and UI feedback (loading indicators, error messages, and empty states) to clearly communicate system status to users.
    </li><br>
    <li>
      Compare local datasets (arrays and objects) with remote API data, explaining trade-offs in control, reliability, flexibility, and availability.
    </li><br>
    <li>
      Explain how relying on external APIs makes apps part of larger systems and apply defensive design strategies when data is delayed, incomplete, or unavailable.
    </li><br>
  </ul>
</div>

